---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Cosmetic Surgery Cost Data 2026: About This Breakdown Site';
const description = 'Cosmetic Cost Breakdown is published by Radif Partners from 2026 Medicare rate files, line by line, with your own quote: no referral, no loan, no personal data.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Cosmetic Cost Breakdown</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>The gap this site fills</h2>
    <p>Search for the cost of a tummy tuck, a breast augmentation or a facelift and the first results come from lenders, clinics and review platforms. Most give one figure as if it were the price, or a wide range with no date, and end with a consultation form or a credit application. A patient holding a quote cannot tell from them how much of it pays the surgeon, how much the anesthesia and how much the operating room, nor whether each part is usual.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules and public data. It is not a medical practice, a medical society, a surgery center, a drug maker, an insurer, a lender or a referral service. No surgeon or clinic is listed, ranked or recommended, no commission is received and no affiliate link is carried.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we rely on</h2>
    <p>For the operating room, the rate files CMS publishes for surgery centers (<Fragment set:html={src('cms_asc_addenda', 'Addendum AA')} />) and hospital outpatient departments (<Fragment set:html={src('cms_opps_b', 'Addendum B')} />), October 2026 editions, with the FY 2026 wage index of each of the {K.METROS.length} metro areas. For anesthesia, the base units of each anesthesia code and the 2026 conversion factor of each Medicare locality (<Fragment set:html={src('cms_anes', 'CMS Anesthesiologists Center')} />). For the surgeon, nothing: no public file publishes surgeons' fees for cosmetic work, so that line comes only from the quote you type. For Botox, the units on the FDA label and the CMS payment limit of the toxin. For the weeks after, the Bureau of Labor Statistics median weekly earnings and the Federal Reserve's G.19 credit rates.</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>It describes bills, never treatments: whether an operation suits you, and how safe it is for you, are questions for a board-certified surgeon who has examined you. Procedures without a CMS code to price the room and the anesthesia, such as hair transplants, neck lifts and dermal fillers, are not estimated rather than guessed. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> gives every formula and its limits; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a figure or a document, not about your own surgery.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
